# Accordion

Let people inspect several related sections without losing the surrounding page.

## Keep grouped behaviour distinct

The [disclosure decision guide](details.html#choose-an-optional-disclosure) covers when to use Details, expanders or an accordion. This page owns the grouped enhanced interaction: section buttons, expanded-state announcements and show-all behaviour. Its readable unenhanced content differs from a browser’s native `details` control.

Retain the upstream component’s one state owner. Do not add competing custom toggles or assume that a visual expander can replace the accordion script. Test both individual and show-all controls, and preserve all readable content when enhancement is unavailable.

## When to use

Use an accordion for optional troubleshooting topics that a person can select independently, such as connection, power and reporting intervals. Test whether headings and a short contents list would work first. Hidden sections increase the effort needed to discover information.

Keep the current state and the next required action outside the accordion. Do not hide a warning, a failed save or a required question. Never put one accordion inside another.

